Harbour Drone
=============

Laptop shows a 3D harbour; a phone flies the drone over the internet; real Trincomalee wind pushes it.

  /             laptop world (Three.js)
  /controller/  phone controller (two touch sticks + reset)

Setup
-----
1. npm install
2. Create a Supabase project, copy .env.example to .env and fill in the URL and anon key
   (anon / publishable key only, never the service-role key).
3. npm run dev        (laptop: http://localhost:5173/)

The QR code points at /controller/ on whatever address the laptop page was opened with, so
to fly from a phone before deploying, open the "Network" URL that Vite prints (same Wi-Fi)
instead of localhost. Without Supabase keys the world still runs on the keyboard alone.

  npm run build       type-check and build to dist/
  npm run preview     serve the built site locally

Deploy
------
npx vercel --prod, with VITE_SUPABASE_URL and VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY set as environment
variables in the Vercel project. Open the site on the laptop and scan the QR code with the phone.

Controls
--------
Phone:    left stick = altitude, right stick = move, RESET
Keyboard: W/S = altitude, arrows = move, R = reset (works without Supabase)

Up on the right stick (or the up arrow) flies north, away from the camera.

Game
----
Goal: take off from the helipad, fly through the three rings (+100 each) and land gently
back on the pad (+100, plus a point per second left) before the 60-second clock runs out.

- The clock starts when the drone lifts off, not when the page loads.
- Rings count when flown through north-south, in either direction, and turn green.
- A landing only counts after climbing at least 1 m, and must be slower than 2 m/s near
  the middle of the pad. Come in faster and the drone bounces: "TOO FAST - EASE DOWN".
- Touching the sea costs 50 and sends you back to the pad; the clock keeps running.
- The flying area is 30 m each way from the pad with a 25 m ceiling.
- After landing or running out of time the round is frozen until RESET, which also
  clears the score.

Wind
----
Current wind speed and direction for Trincomalee harbour (8.59N 81.22E) come from
Open-Meteo, refreshed every minute; no API key needed. Let go of the sticks and the wind
carries the drone. A drone sitting on the pad is not moved.

The compass in the top-right corner and the yellow arrow above the drone both point the way
the wind is pushing, and the arrow above the drone grows with wind speed. If the wind can't
be fetched the drone keeps flying on the last reading.

HUD
---
Top left:      phone link status
Top right:     live wind compass, speed and direction
Bottom left:   QR code and URL for the phone controller
Bottom centre: score and time left
Bottom right:  altitude (m) and speed (m/s)

How it works
------------
The phone broadcasts its stick positions on a Supabase Realtime channel, 20 times a second
while flying and a slower heartbeat when idle. The laptop runs all the physics and treats a
phone that has been quiet for a second as sticks released. Every laptop and phone using the
same Supabase project shares the one channel, so run one game at a time per project.

  index.html             laptop page and HUD
  controller/index.html  phone page
  src/main.ts            input, physics, scoring, HUD updates
  src/world.ts           scene: sky, sea, quay, helipad, rings, drone, wind arrow
  src/controller.ts      touch sticks and broadcasting
  src/realtime.ts        Supabase channel (null when the keys are missing)
  src/wind.ts            Open-Meteo fetch and compass names
  src/styles.css         styles for both pages

Flight feel (acceleration, drag, wind strength, round length, landing speed) is set by the
constants at the top of src/main.ts; ring and pad positions are at the top of src/world.ts.
